/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Merge K Sorted Lists
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Linked List
Difficulty : Hard

Problem:
You are given an array of k sorted linked lists. Merge all
the linked lists into a single sorted linked list and return
its head.

Approach:
Use a Min Heap (Priority Queue) to efficiently keep track of
the smallest current node among all linked lists.

Initially, insert the first node of every non-empty linked
list into the min heap. Repeatedly extract the smallest node,
append it to the merged list, and if that node has a next
node, insert it into the heap.

Continue this process until the heap becomes empty.

Why does this work?

Since each linked list is already sorted, the smallest
unprocessed node from every list is always at its head.

The min heap efficiently identifies the overall smallest node
among all current heads in O(log k) time, where k is the
number of linked lists. By continuously removing the smallest
node and inserting its successor, the merged list remains
sorted throughout the process.

Algorithm:
1. Create a min heap.
2. Insert the head of every non-empty linked list.
3. Create a dummy node for the merged list.
4. While the heap is not empty:
   - Remove the smallest node.
   - Append it to the merged list.
   - If the removed node has a next node, insert it into the heap.
5. Return the merged list.

Time Complexity : O(N log k)

Reason:
Each of the N nodes is inserted into and removed from the
priority queue exactly once. Each heap operation takes
O(log k) time.

Space Complexity : O(k)

Reason:
The priority queue stores at most one node from each linked
list at any time.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

struct Compare {

    // Creates a min heap based on node values.
    bool operator()(ListNode* firstNode, ListNode* secondNode) {
        return firstNode->val > secondNode->val;
    }
};

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* mergeKLists(vector<ListNode*>& lists) {

        // Min heap storing the current smallest node
        // from each linked list.
        priority_queue<
            ListNode*,
            vector<ListNode*>,
            Compare
        > minHeap;

        // Insert the head of every non-empty list.
        for (ListNode* head : lists) {
            if (head != nullptr) {
                minHeap.push(head);
            }
        }

        // Dummy node simplifies list construction.
        ListNode* dummy = new ListNode(-1);
        ListNode* tail = dummy;

        // Merge all linked lists.
        while (!minHeap.empty()) {

            // Get the smallest available node.
            ListNode* currentNode = minHeap.top();
            minHeap.pop();

            // Append it to the merged list.
            tail->next = currentNode;
            tail = tail->next;

            // Insert the next node from the same list.
            if (currentNode->next != nullptr) {
                minHeap.push(currentNode->next);
            }
        }

        ListNode* mergedHead = dummy->next;
        delete dummy;

        return mergedHead;
    }
};
